By the beginning of the 20th century, shotguns were standard equipment for most police forces.

236
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:16,060
and the Winchester Repeating Arms Company led the way with its pump-action model, introduced in 1897.

237
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:24,240
This became, almost overnight, one of the most popular shotguns in the history of this country,

238
00:18:24,600 --> 00:18:27,800
and it was especially popular with police departments,

239
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:34,860
because here you had a multi-shot pump-action shotgun available with a short barrel

240
00:18:34,861 --> 00:18:39,980
that could be used by police agencies for almost any purpose.

241
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:43,380
Now, the primary consideration was for riot control

242
00:18:43,381 --> 00:18:47,560
or when dealing with very dangerous or well-armed individuals.

243
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:53,860
It's easy for any officer to handle, to operate, point it, shoot it,

244
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:55,500
and he would get the message across.

245
00:18:56,460 --> 00:19:02,100
This particular shotgun was purchased by the Omaha, Nebraska Police Department in 1922.

246
00:19:04,860 --> 00:19:10,200
This police video demonstrates why the shotgun is effective for crowd and riot control.

247
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:16,160
As distance increases, the scatter pattern becomes wider,

248
00:19:16,760 --> 00:19:20,700
enabling an officer to exhibit the threat of force over a greater area.

249
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:30,740
With a capacity of seven rounds, the shotgun can adequately meet the demands of almost any situation.

250
00:19:30,741 --> 00:19:38,180
today the remington 870 is the standard choice for police around the world

251
00:19:38,181 --> 00:19:45,420
we haven't chose to turn it in for anything else in 25 years i think it's been very effective

252
00:19:45,421 --> 00:19:51,820
as a matter of fact i think personally my personal opinion is that the 870 is one of the best police

253
00:19:51,821 --> 00:19:57,940
weapons that's out there it's really wood stood the test of time it's a workhorse it's a workhorse

254
00:19:57,941 --> 00:20:01,931
weapon. These weapons tend to stay in arsenals for many, many years.

255
00:20:01,932 --> 00:20:03,340
They can take a beating.

256
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:08,600
If the bad guy is in range, and so are you, if he's equipped with the weapon himself.

257
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:12,760
So this might give you a little bit of an edge as far as you be able to create a little more

258
00:20:12,761 --> 00:20:17,723
space between yourself and the other person who's armed, and a little more accuracy.

259
00:20:17,724 --> 00:20:18,424
And

260
00:20:18,425 --> 00:20:22,817
if anything else, there's a great psychological impact just on that sound.

261
00:20:22,818 --> 00:20:24,080
And a lot of times,

262
00:20:24,081 --> 00:20:29,280
just that sound can cause the other person to rethink their position and maybe decide to end

263
00:20:29,281 --> 00:20:33,853
the encounter without having to go to that next level.

264
00:20:33,854 --> 00:20:37,240
But sometimes even the power of shotguns

265
00:20:37,241 --> 00:20:43,880
hasn't been enough. Nowhere was this more horribly dramatized than in Austin, Texas,

266
00:20:43,881 --> 00:20:46,024
on August 1st, 1966.

267
00:20:46,025 --> 00:20:53,100
On that warm summer day, Charles Whitman, a 25-year-old ex-Marine,

268
00:20:53,101 --> 00:20:57,880
dragged a footlocker filled with guns and ammunition to the tower of the University of Texas.

269
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:05,480
After barricading himself in, Whitman picked up a high-powered rifle and began a killing rampage,

270
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:08,240
which lasted 96 terrifying minutes.

271
00:21:10,340 --> 00:21:14,440
Police officers, armed only with handguns, were essentially helpless.

272
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:21,800
Citizens scrambled to the scene with privately owned hunting rifles and began to fire back.

273
00:21:23,100 --> 00:21:30,260
By the time a police officer finally broke through the barricade, Whitman had killed 16 people and wounded 31.

274
00:21:31,940 --> 00:21:38,040
Police departments around the country, shocked by the incident, were prompted to form their first SWAT teams.

275
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:46,480
Soon, high-powered sniper rifles to combat terrorists like Whitman would become standard in their arsenals.

276
00:21:46,481 --> 00:21:54,740
This weapon here is a Sig Sauer model SSG 3000.

277
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:57,780
It's chambered in .308 caliber.

278
00:21:58,500 --> 00:22:04,260
It's a bolt-action weapon, which means there's a magazine that usually holds an average of five rounds.

279
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:07,380
They do vary, and one round can be chambered at a time.

280
00:22:07,760 --> 00:22:10,980
This weapon can be fired at several hundred yards accurately.

281
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:13,220
You have a Leupold.

282
00:22:13,860 --> 00:22:15,600
It's a 1x10 power scope.

283
00:22:15,601 --> 00:22:19,960
You know, everybody thinks, yeah, you're a sniper, you're way out away from the action,

284
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:22,800
but this is probably one of the most important jobs on the team

285
00:22:22,801 --> 00:22:26,020
because they let us know via the radio what they're seeing,

286
00:22:26,300 --> 00:22:30,480
and they're actually our eyes for us when we're approaching to do some type of tactical maneuver.

287
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:34,580
To address the need for increased firepower,

288
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:39,560
many departments are also training their officers in the use of military-style assault rifles.

289
00:22:39,561 --> 00:22:45,760
The M16 was adopted by the Army during the Vietnam War.

290
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:54,480
In fully automatic mode, it has a cyclic rate of between 700 and 950 rounds per minute.

291
00:22:56,140 --> 00:23:00,560
This weapon here is another mainstay on the Mass State Police stop team.

292
00:23:00,561 --> 00:23:10,820
What this is is a Colt model M16, M4, chambered in .223 caliber or .556 millimeter.

293
00:23:11,360 --> 00:23:16,700
We have a shortened down barrel on this weapon as well as a retractable stock.

294
00:23:16,920 --> 00:23:20,780
It can make it shorter, longer, depending on what you're using it for.

295
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:25,020
We also have a different sighting system on this weapon.

296
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:29,660
It's a red dot sighting system, which is what we consider point of aim, point of impact.

297
00:23:30,440 --> 00:23:33,660
Using this sight, it's simply put the red dot on your target,

298
00:23:34,220 --> 00:23:37,500
and where that red dot is is where your bullet's going to hit.

299
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:43,660
High-powered sniper and assault rifles are giving law enforcement officers

300
00:23:43,661 --> 00:23:47,820
a much-needed edge in combating some of the most violent criminal elements.

301
00:23:49,220 --> 00:23:53,080
But recently, some perpetrators have become much bolder,

302
00:23:53,680 --> 00:23:56,300
often demonstrating a level of mayhem and destruction

303
00:23:56,301 --> 00:24:00,140
reminiscent of one of the most lawless periods in American history,

304
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:02,180
the Roaring Twenties.

305
00:24:03,640 --> 00:24:07,340
Over six million Remington 870s have been made,

306
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:10,880
making it the best-selling shotgun of any type in history.

307
00:24:11,580 --> 00:24:13,080
Modern Marvels will be right back.

308
00:24:15,700 --> 00:24:19,380
In 1920, the 18th Amendment was passed,

309
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:22,240
outlawing the manufacture and sale of alcohol.

310
00:24:22,241 --> 00:24:29,240
Prohibition gave birth to a new breed of criminal, the bootlegger.

311
00:24:29,241 --> 00:24:35,240
It also sparked an unprecedented wave of urban violence.

312
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:46,240
As gangland wars erupted, police departments tried to keep the country dry.

313
00:24:46,241 --> 00:24:53,240
Daily raids destroyed massive amounts of illegal alcohol.

314
00:24:53,241 --> 00:24:59,240
However, police often found themselves outmanned and outgunned.

315
00:24:59,241 --> 00:25:07,240
It was an era that is often defined by a single weapon, the Thompson submachine gun.

316
00:25:07,241 --> 00:25:15,240
Though created for the military, the gun was largely ignored until it became associated with notorious outlaws like John Dillinger.

317
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:17,760
Pretty Boy Floyd

318
00:25:17,761 --> 00:25:20,200
Machine Gun Kelly

319
00:25:20,201 --> 00:25:22,620
and Al Capone

320
00:25:22,621 --> 00:25:28,080
Incredibly, owning a Tommy gun was not that difficult

321
00:25:28,081 --> 00:25:34,680
Anyone with $225 could purchase one by mail order

322
00:25:34,681 --> 00:25:36,700
or from the local hardware store

323
00:25:36,701 --> 00:25:41,320
With a firing rate of 800 rounds per minute

324
00:25:41,321 --> 00:25:44,000
it gave criminals an unheard of advantage

325
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:49,140
There was only one way to even the odds

326
00:25:49,141 --> 00:25:52,440
Soon law enforcement organizations nationwide

327
00:25:52,441 --> 00:25:55,920
Were outfitting their officers and agents with Tommy guns

328
00:25:55,921 --> 00:25:59,640
But the Thompson did have its limitations

329
00:25:59,641 --> 00:26:03,920
In a gun battle with Bonnie and Clyde

330
00:26:03,921 --> 00:26:06,020
Famed Texas lawman Ted Hinton

331
00:26:06,021 --> 00:26:09,660
Found that the Tommy gun was not as all-powerful as advertised

332
00:26:09,661 --> 00:26:13,240
Bonnie and Clyde were able to escape unscathed

333
00:26:13,241 --> 00:26:20,240
Later, in the climatic shootout with the infamous couple, Hinton and his deputies took no chances.

334
00:26:20,241 --> 00:26:34,241
A Browning automatic rifle, a Remington semi-automatic rifle, a 12-gauge Remington shotgun, and two .45 Colt automatics left Bonnie and Clyde dead and their car in

335
00:26:35,491 --> 00:26:36,240
tatters.

336
00:26:43,240 --> 00:26:49,880
Though it was not always preferred, the Thompson did gain some acceptance with both law enforcement

337
00:26:49,881 --> 00:26:54,200
and the military.

338
00:26:54,201 --> 00:26:58,960
American soldiers carried it as recently as World War II in the Vietnam era, and it was

339
00:26:58,961 --> 00:27:03,960
used by various police departments until the 1980s.

340
00:27:03,961 --> 00:27:09,480
You could put a magazine or a clip into the Thompson and just fire off 40, 50, 75 rounds

341
00:27:09,481 --> 00:27:11,280
in a few seconds.

342
00:27:11,281 --> 00:27:12,840
They were part of the armament.

343
00:27:12,841 --> 00:27:15,960
I joined the Los Angeles Police Department in 1961.

344
00:27:15,961 --> 00:27:18,720
I can remember the .45 Thompson submachine guns

345
00:27:18,721 --> 00:27:20,160
being in the gun locker.

346
00:27:20,161 --> 00:27:23,580
They were used by officers when they went out on arrests.

347
00:27:23,581 --> 00:27:25,600
I don't know if they were ever actually used.

348
00:27:25,601 --> 00:27:27,840
More of a show of strength, probably.

349
00:27:29,980 --> 00:27:32,440
This is a Thompson submachine gun.

350
00:27:32,441 --> 00:27:35,600
It's a .48 ACP caliber.

351
00:27:35,601 --> 00:27:39,300
It was manufactured in Bridgeport, Connecticut.

352
00:27:39,301 --> 00:27:48,000
This happens to be a 1928 A1 model that the Massachusetts State Police got briefly after the Second World War.

353
00:27:48,001 --> 00:27:56,620
They were given to the State Police by the National Guard, and for the most part, the Thompson submachine gun was kept in the prison stations.

354
00:27:56,760 --> 00:27:59,620
That would be barracks where a prison was nearby.

355
00:28:00,620 --> 00:28:07,780
If there was a riot or something along those lines, that this particular weapon would be used in quelling the riot.

356
00:28:07,781 --> 00:28:13,260
If there was somebody escaping from police, a suspect that was in the woods or somebody who was dangerous,

357
00:28:13,620 --> 00:28:15,400
this gun would be used in that application.

358
00:28:17,020 --> 00:28:20,320
Today, the Tommy gun is considered technically obsolete.

359
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,720
Yet its history illustrates an ongoing struggle for law enforcement.

360
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:27,240
How to keep up with the bad guys.

361
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:31,440
Even now, 70 years after Prohibition,

362
00:28:31,780 --> 00:28:36,700
the police can still find themselves lagging behind the firepower employed by hardened criminals.

363
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:44,460
This was especially true in the suburban Los Angeles city of North Hollywood in March of 1997.

364
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:50,540
When two heavily armed gunmen were foiled during an attempted bank heist,

365
00:28:50,900 --> 00:28:53,600
a terrifying shootout ensued in the city streets.

366
00:28:57,560 --> 00:29:00,860
The event was televised live across the nation.

367
00:29:02,260 --> 00:29:05,380
The bank robbers seemed determined to stop at nothing.

368
00:29:05,381 --> 00:29:10,540
They wore protective clothing, including bulletproof vests,

369
00:29:10,860 --> 00:29:14,500
and they were armed with a cache of powerful automatic weapons.

370
00:29:15,300 --> 00:29:18,220
The police were seriously outgunned.

371
00:29:19,400 --> 00:29:23,280
The Los Angeles Police Department is like any major police department.

372
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:27,620
Until you have a major event, like say for them,

373
00:29:27,720 --> 00:29:29,920
like in this case the North Hollywood Bank shootout,

374
00:29:29,921 --> 00:29:37,920
Until the event comes up, they realize that the officers with the .38 were undergunned.

375
00:29:37,921 --> 00:29:41,920
They also realize that in this particular case, these suspects were wearing a body armament.

376
00:29:41,921 --> 00:29:44,920
Hopefully that never happens again, but it could.

377
00:29:44,921 --> 00:29:48,920
And so in effect, the weapons they were firing at them were totally ineffective.

378
00:29:51,920 --> 00:29:55,920
Officers on the scene scrambled to find weapons that could even the odds.

379
00:29:55,921 --> 00:30:02,920
just to look at the situation that happened in LA with the bank robbery there and they were

380
00:30:02,921 --> 00:30:07,500
better armed than the police and from the reports that I heard the police were going at the gun

381
00:30:07,501 --> 00:30:13,860
stores and borrowing weapons of this type to go out and engage the threat to kind of balance the

382
00:30:13,861 --> 00:30:20,280
firepower out there the bank robbers were eventually stopped but the damage had been

383
00:30:20,281 --> 00:30:25,486
done. Both gunmen were dead and 18 people had been wounded.

384
00:30:25,487 --> 00:30:27,780
However, a valuable lesson

385
00:30:27,781 --> 00:30:32,760
had been learned and the general consensus was for a need to better arm law enforcement

386
00:30:32,761 --> 00:30:33,697
officers.

387
00:30:33,698 --> 00:30:41,920
Never ever again do I want to see officers of the Los Angeles Police Department

388
00:30:41,921 --> 00:30:42,876
outgunned.

389
00:30:42,877 --> 00:30:50,140
Never ever again do I want to see them having to rush to a gun shop in order

390
00:30:50,141 --> 00:30:55,020
to be able to equalize the firepower of the thugs that they are opposing.

391
00:30:57,420 --> 00:31:02,120
With that in mind, advanced weapons such as the Heckler and Cock MP5

392
00:31:02,121 --> 00:31:05,140
are being issued to police departments across the nation.

393
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:09,400
This gun that I'm about to shoot is the MP5.

394
00:31:10,120 --> 00:31:12,120
It has a retractable stock.

395
00:31:12,900 --> 00:31:15,480
The only accessories that it has on is a surefire light.

396
00:31:15,481 --> 00:31:20,740
It has the Navy trigger group that will go on safe, semi-auto, and in fully auto.

397
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:26,180
I will load two 30-round magazines that are side-by-side in a magazine clamp,

398
00:31:26,340 --> 00:31:30,440
and I will fire two three-round bursts initially.

399
00:31:37,520 --> 00:31:41,720
The MP5 is now used by many local law enforcement agencies,

400
00:31:41,721 --> 00:31:44,720
as well as by the FBI and the Secret Service.

401
00:31:45,480 --> 00:31:54,400
This member of the MP5 family is the MP5 SD, SD4 being suppressed.

402
00:31:54,880 --> 00:31:59,820
The difference here is that it has a suppressor on it, and it should be reasonably a quiet gun to fire.

403
00:32:00,500 --> 00:32:07,560
The application of this gun here is oftentimes a tactical team making an approach on a house.

404
00:32:08,260 --> 00:32:13,980
Typically, narcotic salespeople will occupy the house or the yard with a sentry dog.

405
00:32:13,981 --> 00:32:17,660
This tool here could be used to neutralize a sentry dog.

406
00:32:18,120 --> 00:32:25,140
Some agencies just like to be operating in an environment where there is not the reported sounds of gunshots.

407
00:32:25,560 --> 00:32:28,860
In other words, any reported gunshots would be by a suspect, perhaps.

408
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:36,140
A sound suppressor can also be an effective tool in allowing police officers to maintain an element of surprise.

409
00:32:36,141 --> 00:32:47,780
but the impressive power of an mp5 can be measured in other ways as well

410
00:32:47,781 --> 00:32:51,680
most notably by the success stories of officers in the field

411
00:32:51,681 --> 00:33:00,760
in this particular case there were three state troopers we were acting in the capacity as being

412
00:33:00,761 --> 00:33:02,592
significant drug distributors.

413
00:33:02,593 --> 00:33:06,500
We were working out of a stretch limousine making our deliveries

414
00:33:06,501 --> 00:33:13,480
and in order to give credibility, more credibility, to the foundation that had already been laid

415
00:33:13,481 --> 00:33:19,880
and to help ensure the safety of the officers, we let it be known that during the course of

416
00:33:19,881 --> 00:33:23,900
our deliveries, we routinely not only carry weapons, but we carry semi-automatic weapons

417
00:33:23,901 --> 00:33:29,488
and machine guns. This particular weapon right here is an H&K MP5K.

418
00:33:29,489 --> 00:33:31,740
This is the weapon that was

419
00:33:31,741 --> 00:33:34,524
used in one of those undercover operations.

420
00:33:34,525 --> 00:33:38,280
And it was worn, configured in a shoulder rig on a swivel,

421
00:33:39,340 --> 00:33:42,920
hanging right underneath the right arm, and it was concealed by a sport coat.

422
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:47,960
When the wind blew the sport coat open, they could see it was there, and it spoke volumes.

423
00:33:47,961 --> 00:33:49,220
And it did two things.

424
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:55,440
The first thing it did was it helped ensure the safety of the officers involved in that operation,

425
00:33:55,840 --> 00:33:59,520
because you're certainly going to think twice before you try any aggressive action.

426
00:34:00,340 --> 00:34:06,480
And the second part of it was it gave credibility to the foundation that we had already laid about being serious drug distributors.

427
00:34:06,960 --> 00:34:11,360
And the end result of it was a significant arrest, seizure of a large amount of money.

428
00:34:11,361 --> 00:34:20,800
Submachine guns like the MP5 are the latest example of how police must remain on the cutting edge of weapons technology

429
00:34:20,801 --> 00:34:23,580
in order to stay one step ahead of the criminals.

430
00:34:26,420 --> 00:34:31,860
When Sergeant Ken Cunningham of the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department goes to work each day,

431
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:35,620
he never knows what types of violent situations he might encounter.

432
00:34:35,621 --> 00:34:43,360
The most dangerous scenarios could find Cunningham too undergun to meet a challenge to his authority.

433
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:49,880
His life, as well as the lives of innocent civilians, could hang in the balance.

434
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:56,940
Cunningham patrols his beat with a sophisticated assortment of lethal and non-lethal weapons.

435
00:34:58,620 --> 00:35:01,560
All of it geared to help save lives.

436
00:35:01,561 --> 00:35:08,280
The typical handgun that law enforcement is using right now in the 90s is an autoloader.

437
00:35:08,281 --> 00:35:12,360
That's basically what the LA County Sheriff's Department uses.

438
00:35:12,361 --> 00:35:18,440
The autoloader we use is the 9mm, as manufactured by Beretta.

439
00:35:18,441 --> 00:35:25,280
When I'm on duty and carrying this weapon, typically I will carry two magazines on me.

440
00:35:25,281 --> 00:35:31,080
One magazine on each pouch for a total of three magazines or 46 rounds.

441
00:35:31,081 --> 00:35:33,800
And that's pretty much the law enforcement standard.

442
00:35:38,060 --> 00:35:41,380
This is our standard-issue shotgun for the sheriff's department.

443
00:35:41,580 --> 00:35:43,420
We use it at all our patrol stations.

444
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:48,340
This is a 12-gauge pump shotgun manufactured by the Ithaca Company.

445
00:35:48,940 --> 00:35:53,180
There's nothing that is more scary than hearing that sound

446
00:35:53,181 --> 00:35:57,060
and the bad guys know you're coming and that you got this thing.

447
00:35:57,061 --> 00:36:04,940
And I've seen people's hands go up in the air and guns hit the ground real quick just when they hear that noise because they know what it is.

448
00:36:05,520 --> 00:36:08,260
They have some respect for it. They pretty much know what it can do.

449
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:10,100
They don't want to argue with a shotgun.

450
00:36:16,980 --> 00:36:24,920
In the trunk of Cunningham's patrol car is an impressive arsenal of weapons, which can help defuse almost any violent confrontation.

451
00:36:27,060 --> 00:36:32,100
This is a less than lethal force option that the sheriff's department utilizes.

452
00:36:33,240 --> 00:36:36,640
It shoots a round, a 12-gauge shotgun round.

453
00:36:36,800 --> 00:36:38,660
It's called a flexible baton.

454
00:36:39,100 --> 00:36:44,900
This is none other than a round of ammunition with essentially a small beanbag inside of it.

455
00:36:45,220 --> 00:36:49,940
People that these have been used on, they describe it as being hit with a baseball.

456
00:36:50,720 --> 00:36:56,040
They say it's the same thing as if somebody wound up and threw them a good 60, 70 mile an hour pitch

457
00:36:56,041 --> 00:36:57,520
and hit him in the chest with a baseball.

458
00:36:59,840 --> 00:37:03,740
Okay, the next one I have, it's a less than lethal alternative.

